            {
                "entry_id": "8d26718f-8ccf-3fd6-af37-66e611641c7a",
                "word": "désassimilation",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "8bf9f6d7-8e10-4c7b-9b6c-d184fbf91e6c",
                        "entry_id": "8d26718f-8ccf-3fd6-af37-66e611641c7a",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "désassimilations"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "869bc239-c666-3524-b415-af81259e6a6e",
                        "entry_id": "8d26718f-8ccf-3fd6-af37-66e611641c7a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Travail par lequel une espèce de composé, faisant partie de la substance de l’organisme, s’en sépare et cesse de participer aux actes qu’elle accomplit."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "09476e22-1080-40d1-ab48-765a92c60812",
                                "sense_id": "869bc239-c666-3524-b415-af81259e6a6e",
                                "text": "Il est une autre désassimilation, indépendante de l’apport alimentaire et qui correspond à la destruction des albumines organiques par l’usure; c’est la désassimilation endogène qui se sépare de la précédente par la nature même de ses déchets."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "bfd527a1-ffc3-4546-9573-75a64f336251",
                        "source_entry_id": "8d26718f-8ccf-3fd6-af37-66e611641c7a",
                        "source_sense_id": "869bc239-c666-3524-b415-af81259e6a6e",
                        "target_lang_word": "disassimilation",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "disassimilation",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "35b57297-424e-37b9-a9b2-61f89dd8e1a6",
                                        "entry_id": "dae7d2fc-ce3e-317b-886d-10d4a5f66a43",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "catabolism (metabolism with the release of energy)"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "54f013a5-fa40-4883-9204-bffe7d4bc192",
                                                "sense_id": "35b57297-424e-37b9-a9b2-61f89dd8e1a6",
                                                "text": "It shows that the brain is not important to the proper performance of the functions of assimilation and disassimilation—or those of organic life."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3dddf89f-5f71-4393-b77f-fc15acfbdbf8",
                                                "sense_id": "35b57297-424e-37b9-a9b2-61f89dd8e1a6",
                                                "text": "His experiments show “that about four-fifths of our disassimilations are the result of transformations within the body, comparable to the oxidation of alcohol, and that the remaining one-fifth of the disassimilations are formed at the expense of the living tissues themselves, free of all demands on foreign oxygen.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "184b2f06-bc24-417b-8137-94b7b72c5f0b",
                                                "sense_id": "35b57297-424e-37b9-a9b2-61f89dd8e1a6",
                                                "text": "There is a white-black substance which when acted upon by the visible rays of light undergoes disassimilation and sets up nerve impulses that arouse in the brain the sensation of white."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f337ebd9-c9d3-377f-8dc8-fc1a69bd083c",
                                        "entry_id": "dae7d2fc-ce3e-317b-886d-10d4a5f66a43",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The act of becoming less assimilated or integrated, particularly of ethnic groups."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3ab4dc7e-4e72-4563-8ab7-54cd8a29d086",
                                                "sense_id": "f337ebd9-c9d3-377f-8dc8-fc1a69bd083c",
                                                "text": "Judging from the autobiographical texts of these three authors, Natives often mixed assimilation with a degree of disassimilation."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d9809c1e-6ffc-4c81-8ce0-58fdb8f580c6",
                                                "sense_id": "f337ebd9-c9d3-377f-8dc8-fc1a69bd083c",
                                                "text": "At this point, \"a process of disassimilation begins. The arts, life, and ideals of the nationality become central and paramount; ethnic and national differences change in status from disadvantages to distinctions.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd1df860-9205-4e31-b8dc-bb406c55d466",
                                                "sense_id": "f337ebd9-c9d3-377f-8dc8-fc1a69bd083c",
                                                "text": "I see Jewish Renewal as a late twentieth-century articulation of what I called the second stage of disassimilation of American Jews, its constructive/ illustrative phase."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d1ffb8ac-f424-4b20-8079-5fb30af20ac0",
